# Adjective closed-class bits (%include'd from sv.gram).
#
# Regular adjectives (snäll, grön, ...) inflect via the Adj-reg paradigm in
# morph.gram (gender/number/definiteness agreement, plus -are/-ast comparison).
#
# This file holds what morphology can't derive, namely degree modifiers and
# indeclinable adjectives.

# Degree modifiers ("en mycket stor hund", "ett för stort hus").
mycket   : Deg
för      : Deg
alltför  : Deg
så       : Deg
riktigt  : Deg
väldigt  : Deg
himla    : Deg
otroligt : Deg
jävligt  : Deg
jäkligt  : Deg
jädrigt  : Deg
helt     : Deg
ganska   : Deg
rätt     : Deg

# --- Irregular comparison -----------------------------------------------------
# The comparative is indeclinable throughout ("en större hund", "ett större hus",
# "de större hundarna"), so it sets only `degree`. The superlative keeps the
# strong/weak split that rides on `def`: strong "störst" is predicative ("huset
# är störst"), weak "största" attributive-definite ("det största huset").

# "stor" umlauts (större/störst), which no suffix derives, so its comparison is
# explicit here and the stem sits in Adj-nocomp so the engine never builds
# "*storare". Same treatment as English "good"/better/best.
större  : Adj  <degree>=comp                <lemma>=stor
störst  : Adj  <degree>=sup   <def>=indef   <lemma>=stor
största : Adj  <degree>=sup   <def>=def     <lemma>=stor

# "bra" (good) is indeclinable: it never changes for gender/number/definiteness
# ("en bra hund", "ett bra hus", "de bra hundarna"). Leaving gender/num/strong
# unset lets it unify with any noun. Its comparison is suppletive (bättre/bäst).
bra     : Adj  <degree>=pos                 <lemma>=bra
bättre  : Adj  <degree>=comp                <lemma>=bra
bäst    : Adj  <degree>=sup   <def>=indef   <lemma>=bra
bästa   : Adj  <degree>=sup   <def>=def     <lemma>=bra
